B3 — batch API throughput
2026-04-26T141017Z

## DemangleBatch: existed (channel-based streaming API)

batch.go already had a full channel-based DemangleBatch with:
- Feeder goroutine + N worker goroutines + per-symbol mutex for stats
- BatchRequest / BatchResponse / BatchSummary / BatchOptions types
- Policies: BatchCollect / BatchDrop / BatchPropagate

New additions:
1. DemangleBatchSlice(ctx, []string, workers) []BatchSliceResult
   - Lock-free atomic work-stealing index (sync/atomic.AddInt64)
   - Workers write directly to pre-allocated result slice (no output channel)
   - Falls through to c.Demangle() (auto-detect path)

2. DemangleBatchSliceScheme(ctx, []string, workers, schemeName) []BatchSliceResult
   - Pins all symbols to a single named scheme
   - Resolves scheme once before spawning workers
   - Workers call sch.Demangle() directly — skips per-symbol Sniff loop and
     catalog RLock contention
   - schemeName="" falls back to auto-detect (same as DemangleBatchSlice)

## Bench results (BenchmarkDemangleBatch_1M, pinned swift-stable, 10s)

Machine: 11th Gen Intel Core i9-11900K @ 3.50GHz, 16 physical threads
Corpus: 26,582 pre-parsed symbols (grammar-accepted subset of production corpus)
Input: 1,000,000 symbols (corpus repeated to fill target)

BenchmarkDemangleBatch_1M-16:
  1.13M symbols/sec  (883ms/op, b.N=13)

BenchmarkDemangleBatch_Workers (pinned swift-stable, 200k corpus, 5s):
  workers=1:   243,761 symbols/sec
  workers=2:   431,456 symbols/sec  (1.77x)
  workers=4:   679,659 symbols/sec  (2.79x)
  workers=8:   933,758 symbols/sec  (3.83x)
  (workers=16 from 1M bench: ~1.13M symbols/sec, 4.64x)

## Gate: ≥ 1M symbols/sec

PASSED — 1.13M symbols/sec at 16 workers
Gate threshold: 1,000,000 symbols/sec aggregate (200k × 5 cores)
Actual single-core baseline: 243k/sec (matches B2 235k/sec ±5%)

Note: auto-detect path (DemangleBatchSlice without pinning) achieves ~750k/sec
at 16 workers due to per-symbol Sniff loop cost. Pinned path clears the gate.

